```java
public class Order {
    public String status = "PENDING";
    // anywhere in the codebase:
    // order.status = "SHIPPED";  -- no check that this was ever PAID first
}
```

This compiles and works right up until some code path sets `status` directly without checking the current value — a refund service setting `SHIPPED` on an order that was never `PAID`, say — and there's nothing in the type system or the field itself to stop it.

```java
public class StateMachine<S> {
    private final Map<S, Set<S>> transitions;
    private S current;

    public boolean canTransition(S target) {
        return transitions.getOrDefault(current, Set.of()).contains(target);
    }

    public void transition(S target) {
        if (!canTransition(target)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(current + " -> " + target + " not allowed");
        }
        current = target;
    }
}
```

The transition table is the single source of truth for what's legal — `canTransition` lets a caller check before attempting (useful for disabling a UI button, say), and `transition` enforces the same rule by throwing rather than silently applying an illegal change.

```java
public void ship(Order order) {
    if (!order.isPaid()) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("cannot ship an order that hasn't been paid");
    }
    stateMachine.transition(SHIPPED);
}
```

A **guard** is a condition beyond just "is this edge in the transition graph" — the edge from PAID to SHIPPED might be structurally valid, but a guard adds a business-rule check, like confirming payment actually cleared, before allowing it, layering business logic on top of the pure state-graph check.
